course: everyday
lesson: 8
title: Posture, screens and resting your eyes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your head is heavy, about as heavy as a big bag of flour, and when you bend it far forward over a phone, your neck muscles have to hold it up like a long fishing rod, so they get sore.
explain: Sit with your back supported, feet flat on the floor or a footrest, and the screen about an arm's length away with its top near eye level, so you look straight ahead or a little down.
explain: When you stare at a screen your eyes focus close for a long time and you blink less, so they get tired and dry; the 20-20-20 rule helps: every 20 minutes look at something about 20 feet (6 metres) away for 20 seconds.
explain: Bodies are made to move, so get up and move about every half hour, and play outdoors every day: time in daylight helps protect children's eyes from becoming short-sighted.
explain: A boy in Vancouver playing a game puts a timer on, and when it rings he looks out of the window at the far trees and walks around the room.
explain: A boy in Toronto played on his tablet in bed until midnight every night; he got headaches, fell asleep in class and his marks went down until his family put the tablet away at bedtime.
explain: A girl in Brisbane spent almost every afternoon hunched over a phone indoors; she started to find the board at school blurry, and the eye doctor said she needed glasses and more time outside.
fact: An adult's head weighs about 5 kilograms.
fact: Bending your head far forward over a phone strains the muscles of your neck.
fact: A good screen is about an arm's length away, with its top near eye level.
fact: When you sit, your back should be supported and your feet flat on the floor or a footrest.
fact: People blink less when they stare at a screen, so their eyes can feel dry.
fact: The 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes, look at something 20 feet away for 20 seconds.
fact: Looking at something far away lets the focusing muscles of the eyes relax.
fact: Sitting still for a long time makes muscles stiff, so you should get up and move often.
fact: Playing outdoors in daylight helps protect children's eyes from becoming short-sighted (seeing far-away things blurry).
fact: Bright screens and exciting games just before bed make it harder to fall asleep.
fact: Reading in dim light can make your eyes tired, but it does not damage them for good.
fact: Light from screens late in the evening makes it harder to fall asleep.
fact: Children who spend more time outdoors are less likely to become short-sighted.
exercise: Why does your neck get sore when you look down at a phone for a long time? | Your neck muscles hold up your heavy head | Phones send out rays that hurt necks | Your neck bones get shorter | Your head becomes heavier the longer you look
exercise: How far away should a computer screen be? | About an arm's length | As close as the tip of your nose | On the other side of the room | It does not matter at all
exercise: Where should the top of the screen be? | Near eye level | Far above your head | Down by your knees | Behind your back
exercise: How should your feet be when you sit at a desk? | Flat on the floor or a footrest | Tucked under your bottom | Wrapped tightly around the chair legs | Dangling in the air
exercise: Why do your eyes feel dry after a long time at a screen? | You blink less when you stare | Screens suck water out of your eyes | Screens blow cold air at your eyes | Your tears get used up by reading
exercise: What is the 20-20-20 rule? | Every 20 minutes, look 20 feet away for 20 seconds | Read for 20 minutes, then sleep for 20 hours | Sit 20 centimetres from the screen | Blink 20 times every 20 seconds while you read or play games
exercise: Why does looking at something far away rest your eyes? | The focusing muscles of the eyes relax | It cleans your eyes | It makes your eyes grow bigger and see more | It makes you blink more slowly
exercise: Why should you get up and move every so often? | Sitting still a long time makes muscles stiff | Chairs get too warm | Moving makes the screen brighter | Your bones shrink a little each hour that you sit
exercise: What helps protect children's eyes from becoming short-sighted? | Playing outdoors in daylight | Sitting much closer to the screen | Wearing sunglasses indoors all day | Eating plenty of sugary sweets
exercise: Why is it hard to fall asleep after a game on a bright screen? | Bright light and excitement keep you awake | Games make your bed lumpy and uncomfortable to lie in | Screens make the room cold | Games use up your sleep
exercise: Does reading in dim light damage your eyes for good? | No, it only makes them tired | Yes, it slowly makes you go blind | Yes, it makes your eyes change colour | Yes, but only if you read after dinner
exercise: How heavy is an adult's head, roughly? | About 5 kilograms | Only about 50 grams | About 50 kilograms | About half a kilogram
exercise: Which way of sitting is best for your back? | Back supported against the chair | Slumped down with your back curled | Leaning far forward with no support | Lying sideways over the armrest
test: A girl in Ohio has tired eyes after a long homework session on a tablet. What would help most? | Look out of the window at something far off | Hold the tablet closer to her face so she can see | Make the screen much brighter | Rub her eyes hard
test: Why does the 20-20-20 rule ask you to look far away and not at another close thing? | Far looking lets the focusing muscles relax | Far things are brighter | Close things are bad for your eyes | It is a rule with no reason, made up by somebody long ago
test: Sam's laptop is on his lap and he bends his head right down for hours. What will probably happen? | His neck and shoulders will get sore | He will grow taller | His eyes will change colour | His laptop will get slower and slower until it stops
test: Which family rule would help everyone sleep better? | No screens for about an hour before bed | Play exciting games in bed until you feel tired | Keep the phone on the pillow | Watch films until midnight
test: A doctor tells a family their children should play outside more. Which is a good reason? | Daylight time helps protect children's eyes | Outside air makes eyes darker | Indoor air causes short sight | Outside play is what makes children need glasses
test: Which is true? | You blink less while staring at a screen | You blink more while staring at a screen | Blinking makes your eyes drier | Screens make you blink every second
test: Mia has been sitting still at the computer for two hours and her back is stiff. What is wisest? | Get up, stretch and move around | Sit even more still | Lean further forward towards the screen | Turn up the brightness
test: Why does holding a phone up at eye level help your neck? | Your head stays balanced over your neck | The phone gets lighter | Your arms become stronger from holding it up | It makes the screen bigger
test: A boy reads a comic under the covers with a dim torch. What will probably happen? | His eyes may get tired, but they will be fine | He will need glasses for ever | His eyes will be damaged for good | Nothing at all, because dim light is the best light for reading in
test: been on the ipad ages, eyes feel dry | Look at something far away and blink | Hold it closer to your eyes | Turn the brightness right up | Stare harder at the screen so your eyes stay open
test: 20 20 20 what's that again | Every 20 min look 20 feet away for 20 sec | Read 20 pages, rest 20 min, 20 times | Sit 20 cm from the screen | Play 20 games then stop
test: playing games in bed then cant sleep, why | Bright screens and excitement keep you awake | Games make you hungry | The bed is too soft | Sleep only comes after midnight for children who play games
test: neck hurts from my phone lol | Hold it higher; don't bend your head right down | Bend lower to see better | Hold it in your lap | Use it lying on your tummy
test: eyes r sore and i've got a headache after gaming all day | Stop, rest your eyes and go outside | Turn the brightness up and keep going | Move closer to the screen | Rub your eyes hard and carry on
test: can't sleep, gonna scroll on my phone till i'm tired | Put the phone away, its light keeps you awake | Yes, scrolling makes you sleepy | Yes, if the sound is off | Yes, turn the screen brighter
test: neck hurts from looking down at my phone, why? | Bending your head down strains your neck | Phones send out heat that hurts your neck | Screens make muscles weaker | Your neck is too short for phones
test: my cousin reckons if u read under the covers with a torch ur eyes go blind permanently | That is a rumour; dim light can tire your eyes but does not damage them for good | It is true, reading in dim light blinds you for life | It is true, but only if you read comics | It is true, so you should only read outside
test: apparently Max played his console 14 hours straight and his neck locked and he couldn't turn his head | Whether or not it was 14 hours, long screen time strains your neck, so take breaks and move | It proves consoles break necks every time | It is only a story, so posture never matters | It means only consoles hurt necks, not phones
test: Lily has to wear glasses now and someone called her four-eyes, is it ok to say it too | No, calling her names is unkind; glasses just help her see | Yes, because everyone else is saying it | Yes, as long as you say it as a joke | Yes, but only when she is not listening
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the 20-20-20 rule? | At intervals of twenty minutes, an object twenty feet distant is to be regarded for twenty seconds | At intervals of twenty seconds, the eyes are to be closed for twenty minutes | Twenty minutes of reading are to be followed by twenty hours of rest | The screen is to be placed twenty centimetres from the eyes
exam: Identify the recommended position of a screen relative to the person observing it. | Approximately an arm's length distant, its upper edge near the level of the eyes | As close to the face as possible, its upper edge below the chin | Far beneath the level of the knees, upon the floor | Directly above the head, upon the ceiling
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning time spent by children out of doors in daylight. | It assists in protecting the eyes of children from becoming short-sighted | It renders the eyes of children more liable to short sight | It exerts no influence of any description upon the eyes | It is detrimental to the eyes unless sunglasses be worn
exam: It is given that the eyes feel dry after prolonged regard of a screen. Determine the principal cause thereof. | Persons blink less frequently whilst they regard a screen | The screen emits a vapour that dries the eyes | The eyes produce no tears whatsoever during daylight | The screen draws moisture from the face by heat
